<!-- Ministry Philosophy -->
<section class="content-section alt" id="philosophy">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>Ministry Philosophy</h2>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">Christ-Centered, Not Tribal.</strong>
            I use the phrase "Christ-centered" rather than "Christian" deliberately.
            The word "Christian" has become, for many, a tribal marker&mdash;a way of asking
            "are you one of us?" I want to point to the person of Jesus Christ rather
            than to any institution's claim on Him.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">Plainness Over Complexity.</strong>
            The name "Words of Plainness" comes from {% scripture "2 Nephi 31:3" %}&mdash;the idea that God speaks
            to His children in language they can understand. I believe truth is best served
            by clarity, not obscured by unnecessary complexity.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">Faith Expressed in Living.</strong>
            I believe discipleship flows naturally from love&mdash;not as a transaction to earn
            salvation, but as the heart's natural response to grace. "If ye love me, keep
            my commandments" frames obedience as love's expression, not a checkbox requirement.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">Bridges, Not Walls.</strong>
            Believers across traditions have more in common than the things that separate us.
            This ministry exists to explore that common ground without pretending our
            differences don't exist.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- How This Work Is Made (formerly AI Disclosure; #ai-disclosure kept for footer links) -->
<section class="content-section" id="ai-disclosure">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2 id="how-this-is-made">How This Work Is Made</h2>

        <p>
            If you have wondered whether a human being stands behind these words, I honor the
            question. You should ask it. We live in a day when a machine can produce fluent
            paragraphs about God without knowing Him, and a reader who demands to know who is
            speaking — and whether the speaker believes what he says — is exercising exactly the
            discernment the scriptures require of us: "Prove all things; hold fast that which is
            good" ({% scripture "1 Thessalonians 5:21" %}). So let me answer plainly.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">The witness is mine.</strong>
            I spent twenty-six years teaching science before giving myself to this ministry full
            time. I served the Lord as a missionary in Argentina as a young man, and I serve now as
            an ordained Elder in the Melchizedek Priesthood of The Church of Jesus Christ of
            Latter-day Saints. The theology in these pages, the convictions, the questions, the
            personal reflections, and the testimony that anchors them — these are my own. I learned
            for myself, on my knees, that God lives and that Jesus is my living Savior and Redeemer,
            and everything published here flows from that witness. No machine wrote it, because no
            machine has it.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">Scribes and servants.</strong>
            The Lord's word has always passed through helping hands. Paul dictated his epistle to
            the Romans through a scribe named Tertius ({% scripture "Romans 16:22" %}), yet no one
            doubts whose testimony the epistle carries. Prophets and apostles have worked through
            scribes, translators, and editors in every age. The question that matters is not whose
            hand held the pen but whose witness the words bear — and who answers to God for them.
            Here, that is me. I hold the governing pen, and I hold the accountability.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">The tools I use.</strong>
            I use modern tools openly: Claude, Gemini, ChatGPT, and Copilot for editing, formatting,
            refining, and website code; ElevenLabs and Suno in my music and narration workflow;
            OpenArt and related tools for imagery; and AI-assisted analysis for finding patterns
            across scripture. They serve me the way a level serves a mason — the wall is still the
            mason's work. I have also built my writing process so that these tools learn to follow
            <em>my</em> voice, drawn from decades of my own writing, and not the other way around.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">What these tools never do.</strong>
            They do not originate doctrine. They do not write my testimony. They do not publish
            anything. Every AI-assisted draft is painstakingly mediated — and sometimes remediated —
            and verified against authoritative sources before I approve it. If a page carries my
            name, I have read every word of it and I answer for every word of it.
        </p>

        <p>
            The discipleship web apps and the main website are designed to support your own
            studies, not replace your personal engagement with the text.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong class="text-gold">Prove all things.</strong>
            If anything I have published reads untrue to you,
            <a href="/connect/#contact" class="text-gold">write to me</a> and show me where. I will
            answer for it — that is a promise I make as the author, not a policy written by a
            committee. And when you have proven the words, I invite you to do what Philip invited
            Nathanael to do: "Come and see" ({% scripture "John 1:46" %}). The words are only the
            lantern. What they point to is Christ.
        </p>

        <p class="about-signature">
            — Brother Aaron Powner<br>
            <em>Kaysville, Utah · somewhere on the road</em><br>
            <em>Last reviewed: September 2026</em>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section class="content-section alt" id="faq">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Are you trying to convert people to your church?</h3>
        <p>
            No. While I'm transparent about my Latter-day Saint background, this ministry is
            about following Jesus Christ and finding common ground among all who seek Him.
            I'm happy to answer questions about my faith tradition, but my goal is to point
            people to Christ, not to recruit for any institution.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">What if I don't believe in God yet?</h3>
        <p>
            You're welcome here. Honest questions are sacred. I'd rather walk alongside someone
            who's genuinely seeking than speak only to those who already agree with me.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Do I need to agree with you to participate?</h3>
        <p>
            Not at all. Respectful dialogue across differences is exactly what this ministry is
            designed for. I learn from people who see things differently, and I hope you might
            find something useful here even if we disagree on some points.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">What does "Words of Plainness" mean?</h3>
        <p>
            It comes from {% scripture "2 Nephi 31:3" %} in the Book of Mormon: "For my soul delighteth in plainness;
            for after this manner doth the Lord God work among the children of men." The idea is
            that spiritual truth should be communicated clearly&mdash;accessible to everyone, not just
            scholars or insiders.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">How can I support this ministry?</h3>
        <p>
            The best support is engagement&mdash;read, reflect, share what resonates, and
            <a href="/connect/#contact" class="text-gold">reach out</a> if you have questions
            or want to continue the conversation. If you'd like to support in other ways,
            please contact me directly.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- Glossary -->
<section class="content-section" id="glossary">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>Glossary</h2>
        <p>
            Terms that may be unfamiliar to visitors from different faith backgrounds.
            This glossary aims to bridge language differences without assuming everyone
            shares the same vocabulary.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Christ-centered</h3>
        <p>
            Oriented around Jesus Christ as the focus of faith and practice. Used here
            instead of "Christian" to emphasize the person of Christ rather than
            institutional or denominational identity.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Covenant</h3>
        <p>
            A sacred agreement between God and His people. In many Christian traditions,
            covenants involve promises and commitments that shape the disciple's relationship
            with God.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Discipleship</h3>
        <p>
            The practice of following Jesus Christ&mdash;learning from His teachings,
            emulating His example, and growing in faith over time.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Latter-day Saint</h3>
        <p>
            A member of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, sometimes
            informally called "Mormon." The term reflects the church's belief in
            being Christ's covenant people in the latter days before His return.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Melchizedek Priesthood</h3>
        <p>
            In Latter-day Saint theology, the higher priesthood authority, named after
            the ancient priest-king Melchizedek mentioned in Genesis and Hebrews.
            Elders in this priesthood are authorized to perform certain ordinances
            and serve in leadership roles.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Seeker</h3>
        <p>
            Someone exploring questions of faith, meaning, and God&mdash;whether from a
            position of doubt, curiosity, or spiritual awakening.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-gold" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Saint</h3>
        <p>
            In biblical usage, simply a believer&mdash;one who follows Christ. Paul addressed
            his letters to "the saints" at various churches. The term doesn't imply
            perfection but rather dedication to discipleship.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

